Depth entailment weakens across insertion of another token that is itself depth-entailed by the same finite set. If the enlarged set is depth-consistent, then it depth-entails the original token.

The bottom token is the bottom clause for the enlarged set. For an atom, an empty function set of the enlarged set allows the atom weakening along the subset, while a nonempty function set makes the atom set empty, so an atom of the original set cannot sit there. For a function token, a nonempty function set of the enlarged set allows the function-world weakening along the subset. If that function set is empty, the proof cases on the entailment of the inserted token. Insertion of bottom preserves the atom set and the function set, so the empty atom set, the witness subset, and the nonempty function world of the original function-token entailment carry over. An inserted atom cannot lie in the empty atom set of that entailment. An inserted function token places its pair into the function set of the enlarged set, contradicting emptiness.
